SELECT v.id, c.id, $t$Best Bed Racks for 2016–2023 Toyota Tacoma (Deck Rail Mount, Tent-Ready)$t$, $m$Overland bed racks verified for the 3rd-gen Tacoma's deck-rail system, for rooftop tents, bikes and cargo.$m$, $i$The 3rd-gen Tacoma's composite bed has factory deck rails that most racks clamp to — no drilling. Pick by height: low-profile (cab height) for MPG and tents; mid-height for bed access under a tent; high (over-cab) for max storage.$i$, $g$- **Deck rails are the mount** — racks without deck-rail clamps need drilling into the composite bed; avoid.
- **5 ft vs 6 ft bed** need different rack lengths; most listings specify.
- **Rooftop tent load**: the composite bed rail is rated lower than steel — stay under the rack maker's dynamic rating (typically 300–500 lb).
- **Tonneau + rack**: only combo systems allow both.$g$, $f$[{"q": "Do I need to drill?", "a": "No — deck-rail-mount racks clamp into the factory channels."}, {"q": "What height for a rooftop tent?", "a": "Mid-height keeps the tent below the cab roofline for wind and lets you open the tailgate."}, {"q": "Will a 3rd-gen rack fit the 2024 Tacoma?", "a": "No — the 4th-gen bed rails changed. Buy 2024+ listings for the new truck."}]$f$::jsonb, $v$**Budget:** low-profile steel deck-rail rack. **Best overall:** mid-height aluminum. **Premium:** full over-cab system with accessory tracks.$v$, 'draft', CURRENT_DATE
FROM vehicles v JOIN makes m ON m.id=v.make_id JOIN categories c ON c.slug='bed-racks'
